1 |text director alfred green melodrama baby face with barbara stanwyck ranks one the more notorious the pre code movies these films were produced before the production code administration had the power enforce its rules 1934 inspiration scenarist gene markey and midnight mary scribe kathryn scola penned the screenplay based mark canfield story about the rise and fall girl who used her sexual charms acquire wealth and position society incidentally mark canfield was pseudonym for producer daryl zanuck these pre code films today seem tame but they aroused controversy galore and contained more racy material than most movies until the late 1950s when the code began erode the themes that the filmmakers explore are women versus men women versus women and women versus society our crafty protagonist does enough skulduggery that all themes are about equal lily worthless father nick powers robert barret distant drums operates illegal speakeasy bar during prohibition when the thirteen amendment outlawed liquor and brews his own booze still out back nick such obnoxious fellow that pimps out her beautiful but hard working daughter lily barbara stanwyck night nurse but lily refuses help her father out with sleazy local politician the politician sipple arthur hohl private detective vows retaliate for lily refusal accommodate him later nick chews his rebellious daughter out lily reproaches him yeah tramp and who blame father swell start you gave nothing but men dirty rotten men and you lower than any them sooner has she stormed off than nick dies when his still blows and kills him lily and her african american maid chico theresa harris arrowsmith pack their bags and catch ride the first freight leaving town sooner have our heroines arrived new york than lily uses her charm get job bank visually director green shows lily shrewd ascension the ladder with camera angles that move upward until lily sexuality threatens destroy the bank one point lily breaks marriage between one bank offi