1 |text this early film has its flaws predictable plot and some overlong scenes dubious relevance but already clearly demonstrates hitchcock mastery editing and the use powerful images also among the most expressionist his films stylistically note for examples the weird distortions uses during the party sequence and the frequent echoes both title and plot the imagery its core though remains the final match which still among the more exciting examples cinematic boxing even though you know that the hero has win becomes quite believable that will lose and the movement his wife from the champion corner his motivating the final plot pay off very well entwined with the progress the match the inserts the stopwatch exactly what they should you can almost hear the ticking even though this silent film the visuals often have surprisingly auditory feel them the pacing becomes astonishingly rapid and the viewer gets sucked into the excitement and brutality both the match and the sexual jealousy which underlies the only dvd release with which familiar that laserlight public domain company with each hitchcock silent they released they attached various musical selections mostly orchestral the action the sound editing frequently sloppy and the sound quality varies widely but some genuine care seems have gone into most the actual choices and the music accompanying the final match works extremely well unlikely that this sequence will ever better accompanied than here this much more impressive film than its present obscurity would suggest deserves honorable place both the hitchcock canon and the slender list worthwhile boxing films
