1 |text imagine victorian literature slowly sinking into the mire the increasingly distant past pulled down the weight its under skirts along comes television its best has redemptive power and with dramatisations like those the bbc produce finely victorian literature gets new stab life the religious themes the moral overtones may increasingly ill ease world longer easily shocked and acquainted with cohabitation affairs and domestic violence but those old well told stories have enduring power and this one hidden gem hard gauge today just how forceful feminist and extraordinary ann bronte masterpiece the tenant wildfell hall actually was emerging from the primeval slime restrictive corsets xc2 x96 bodily mental societal xc2 x96 her heroine helen huntingdon escapes miserable marriage flees brutality and alcoholism braves not only her abusive husband fury but society pinched intolerance and malicious gossip wreak change her life she pays price but retains her self respect she falls love along the way she emerges battered but victorious and strong just love watching women like these screen the actors are superb xc2 x96 the best brits have offer the love story beautifully handled with real passion and feeling well matched actors tara fitzgerald inhabits every aspect the complicated heroine and has been said here other reviewers less sharply defined and beautiful face could survive that petrifying hairstyle toby stephens striking sparks off her contributes just the right combination headstrong handsome youth and passionate yearning vulnerability rupert graves one favourite british actors ever enjoys himself the charismatic villain much that you almost with him the end one perfect the supporting cast ably create world into which you sink without feeling that coarse compromises have been made modern tastes and without having felt preached another bbc classic highly recommended this how romantic literature should dramatised
