1 |text set the cameroons west africa the 1950s claire denis chocolat beautifully photographed and emotionally resonant tone poem that depicts the effects dying colonialism young family during the last years french rule the theme similar the recent nowhere africa though the films are vastly different scope and emphasis the film told from the perspective adult returning her childhood home foreign country france dalens mireille perrier young woman traveling through cameroon recalls her childhood when her father francois cluzet was government official the french cameroons and she had loving friendship with the brooding manservant prot xc3 xa9e isaach bankol xc3 xa9 the heart the film however revolves around france mother aim xc3 xa9e giulia boschi and her love hate relationship with prot xc3 xa9e that seething with unspoken sexual tension the household divided into public and private spaces the white families rooms are private and off limits all except prot xc3 xa9e who works the house while the servants are forced eat and shower outdoors exposing their naked bronze bodies the white family gazes becomes clear when her husband marc fran xc3 xa7ois cluzet goes away business that aim xc3 xa9e and prot xc3 xa9e are sexually attracted each other but the rules society prevent from being openly acknowledged one telling sequence she invites him into her bedroom help her put her dress and the two stare each other image the mirror with defiant longing their eyes knowing that any interaction taboo the young france cecile ducasse also forms bond with the manservant feeding him from her plate while shows her how eat crushed ants and carries her his shoulders walks beneath the nocturnal sky spite their bond the true nature their master servant relationship apparent when france commands prot xc3 xa9e interrupt his conversation with teacher and immediately take her home and when prot xc3 xa9e stands beside her the dinner table waiting for her next command when plane loses its propeller and forced land the nearby mountains the crew and passengers must move into the compound until replacement part can located each visitor shows their disdain for the africans one wealthy owner coffee plantation brings leftover food from the kitchen his black mistress hiding his room another luc jean claude adelin arrogant white frenchman upsets the racial balance when uses the outside shower eats with the servants and taunts aim xc3 xa9e about her attraction prot xc3 xa9e leading her final emotional confrontation with the manservant chocolat loosely autobiographical adapted from the childhood memories the director and slowly paced and mysterious the brooding isolation the land which filmed denis makes her point about the effects colonialism without preaching romanticizing the characters there are victims oppressors simplistic good guys prot xc3 xa9e servant but also protector when stands guard over the bed where aim xc3 xa9e and her daughter sleep protect them from rampaging hyena sad fact that prot xc3 xa9e treated boy and not man but bankol xc3 xa9 imbues his character with such dignity and stature that lessens the pain because its pace western audiences may have work hard fully appreciate the film and denis does not roger ebert phrase coach our emotions the truth chocolat lies the gestures and glances that touch the silent longing our heart
