1 |text this hard hitting often violent western the peckinpah leone tradition surprisingly directed andrew mclaglen whose previous westerns particularly those that starred john wayne were mainly the john ford mode both surprisingly traditional good guys bad guys and incredible date well heston portrays former captain the arizona territorial police who has been retirement for year having turned over the law enforcement reins reform minded sheriff michael parks and finding his ways enforcing the law being taken over autos telegraphs telephones and the railroad the first years the 20th century but soon confronted with menace from his past half breed outlaw coburn that put away more than decade before for train robbery that killed four guards subsequent shootout coburn wife was killed and coburn out for most nasty sort revenge involves the kidnapping and eventually the rape heston daughter hershey him and his gang the result taut and violent pursuit through the mountains and deserts southern arizona the last hard men based brian garfield novel gun down violent many places including the showdown between heston and coburn and the rape scene involving hershey and two members coburn gang quade paull probably every bit questionable similar scenes straw dogs and deliverance but that doesn detract too terribly much from the film psychological approach the western genre mclaglen able handle the bloody story with significant panache and heston performance aging lawman was probably the best one ever gave any his 1970s films coburn makes for especially cold blooded heavy and both parks and chris mitchum hershey intended husband good turns well the music here cribbed from jerry goldsmith scores 100 rifles and the 1966 remake stagecoach but still works here wisely filmed totally location southeastern arizona and utilizing the old tucson set the last hard men needs released fox vhs and dvd soon western that deserves nothing less
