1 |text emory cincinatti steel worker like his father before him and for most the 20th century the twin pillars his family existence have been the steel mill and the union the mill which once employed 000 has seen its numbers dwindle 000 recently and now the plant just shut its doors leaving single security guard first newly unemployed emory and his pals enjoy their independence hanging out around town and carousing their favorite bar where they down depth charges with reckless abandon they think the mill will reopen after listening their union rep optimistic spiel but reality starts sink when they find themselves selling their personal vehicles struggle put food the table and stave off foreclosure their homes emory father dedicated union man sure the plant will reopen and recalls for his son all the short lived closures during his own years the mill meanwhile some the unemployed men take demeaning make work jobs hop their trucks and take off desperate search for employment finally the union admits its helplessness emory explains his stubborn father that times have changed and that the mill won ever open again emory tearfully asks what did wrong lifetime hard work and devotion job union church and family have left him with nothing and nowhere turn hits rock bottom when drunken rage manhandles his young sons and knocks his wife the floor tossed out his own home and stinging from the plant manager comments that and his men didn work hard enough justify their substantial paychecks emory recruits the steel workers still left town something that will demonstrate all what they are capable early the morning they break into the mill fire the furnaces and work harder than they ever have their lives producing one shift enough high quality steel pipes fill the loading docks from wall wall top bottom something the plant manager thought was impossible arriving the suddenly reopened plant the stupefied manager looks around him the tremendous output that came from single day work realizing that production like this could make the plant profitable again the manager asks emory can you this every day emory forced nod and the manager asks then what were you trying prove emory explains that the workers decades hard work honesty and devotion their jobs had meaning and that showing how much they could produce one day just spit your eye emory bids tearful farewell his wife and kids takes off with his buddies look for work down south promising relocate the family when finds this powerful and honest treatment the plight american workers displaced foreign competition and gives realistic view the costs they bear for the short sightedness concession demanding unions and greedy plant owners who extracted every penny they could from their factories but never gave back modernizing them peter strauss emory john goodman his best friend gary cole his college boy brother pamela reed emory sympathetic wife and john doucette his dying father all turn excellent performances this fine picture
