-1 |text anyone who visited drive ins the 1950s 60s and 70s must have seen film two american international pictures distributor that resembled 1980s giant cannon films wherever movie goers ventured aip would right there supply the latest vogue titles the 50s came horror movies like voodoo woman and the undead the 60s were frankie avalon annette funicello beach comedies and biker flicks like the glory stompers and into the 70s aip churned out grindhouse level trash like cannibal girls and sugar hill dillinger released 1973 one the more highbrow aip efforts that capture the true spirit drive film making one those uneven over the top flicks that satisfied the masses thirst for entertainment craftsmanship and common sense damned the whole dillinger typical for its era entertaining and worth couple hours but certainly not memorable heavy action and short both acting and historical fact dillinger was fair effort screenwriter director john milius magnum force but certainly left room for improvement his extensive career the 109 minute dillinger epic for aip scope follows the quest fbi midwest chief melvin purvis played academy award winner ben johnson purvis was the investigator who sought revenge for four fbi agents killed 1933 kansas city ambush that helped gangster frank nash escape justice large were the men who supposedly plotted that breakout including expert bankrobber john dillinger warren oates pretty boy floyd steve kanaly and psychopath baby face nelson richard dreyfuss dillinger eventually joined forces with floyd and nelson taking along homer van meter harry dean stanton and harry pierpont geoffrey lewis also hooked with billie frechette michelle phillips prostitute french and indian extraction while taking place over several months 1933 dillinger basically chase film with purvis entourage looking run down and kill off the men wanted edgar hoover dillinger has documentary feel listing dates and places while johnson supplies loose narrative purvis milius keeps honest depression look using authentic fashion cars weapons and buildings also sprinkles around black and white photography and stock footage gangster shootouts the film never boring moving quick haphazard pace the action scenes are dillinger strongpoint edited competently fred feitshans his last professional effort thousands blank ammunition rounds must have been used make this film not mention pounds explosives this film certainly not for the squeamish with people getting shot and dropping dead all over the place the violence while gratuitous brings some understanding the mayhem that organized crime dumped american life this film never transcends its exploitation status however because the needed writing just isn there john milius somewhat overrated filmmaker places way too much emphasis action the action scenes mostly blood filled shootouts are impressive and comparable with any major crime film its era including 1967 bonnie and clyde but simply don get know much about dillinger and his gang members people the vital relationship that develops between dillinger and frechette barely touched upon with the pair meeting bar during one scene and cavorting lovers just ten minutes afterward melvin purvis also seems wander and out the storyline becoming prominent figure only when milius needs keep the film from unraveling all too often the film takes shoot persona when its characters could have been explored detail aside from this the picture main crime ignorance historical fact while many say that dillinger just film films such this one that create fables and make them permanent those with knowledge gangster history will point out that john dillinger was not the last his ring die milius screenplay and the film documentary style encourage believe fact dillinger died before baby face nelson and homer van meter also was said not carrying gun the night his death nor did have billie frechette tow while these inaccuracies might make for high drama there reason why milius couldn have stayed with the facts and written great story around them warren oates performance dillinger quite good although sometimes looks unconvincing oates humorous and nicely portrays how dillinger became consumed his larger than life image the american press however never really feel the menace invoked his lifetime ben johnson gives some life purvis suave but rather flat michelle phillips brings emotion the billie frechette character and really too bad that milius screenplay didn flesh out her relationship with dillinger never learn what drew her cold blooded killer other than the stereotype easy going girl who attracted men danger the supporting roles with kanaly dreyfuss stanton lewis and briefly appearing cloris leachman are acceptable for such talent piece 1970s exploitation dillinger appears doomed retail bargain bins which exactly where picked mgm dvd release for the film nicely presented widescreen must for drive flicks with subtitles french and spanish dillinger theatrical trailer supplied lone extra largely forgotten except gangster movie fans and drive enthusiasts the film doesn really call for much else way supplementary material for fans the genre certainly worth checking out out roving reviewer www geocities com paul_johnr
