-1 |text the author numerous novels plays and short stories somerset maugham 1874 1965 was considered among the world great authors during his lifetime and although his reputation has faded over the years his work continues command critical respect and large reading public published 1944 the razor edge the tale world war veteran whose search for spiritual enlightenment flies the face shallow western values was maugham last major novel and was immensely popular given that the novel conflicts are internalized spiritual and philosophical issues was also extremely odd choice for film version but darryl zannuck 20th century fox fell love with the book and snapped the screen rights shortly after publication according film lore the razor edge was directed the legendary george cukor from screenplay maugham himself and does seem that maugham wrote adaptation when the film went into production however cukor was replaced edmund goulding director less known for artistic touch than workman like manner and the maugham script was replaced with one lamar trotti the author such memorable screenplays the oxbow incident tyrone power recently returned from military service during world war was cast the spiritually conflicted larry darrell gene tierney one the great beauties her era was cast socialite isabell bradley the supporting cast was particularly notable including herbert marshall anne baxter clifton webb lucille watson and elsa lanchester both budget and shooting schedule were lavish and when the film debuted 1946 was greatly admired public and critics alike but time has way putting things into perspective seen today the razor edge indeed beautifully produced film but that aside the absolute best one can say for that achieves fairly consistent mediocrity most cases the major problem the script although reasonably close maugham novel terms plot noticeably off the mark terms character and completely fails capture the fundamental issues that drive the story are told that larry search enlightenment are told that receives are told acts but spite the occasional and largely superficial comment are never really told anything about the spiritual artistic philosophical and intellectual processes behind any are most particularly never told anything significant about the nature the enlightenment itself has the effect cutting off the story its knees are left with the shell maugham plot which centers the relationship between larry and isabell woman larry loves but leaves due the growing ideological riff that opens between them tyrone power and gene tierney were more noted for physical beauty than talent but both could turn good performances when they received solid directorial and script support unfortunately that does not happen here they are extremely one note and power greatly miscast boot fortunately the supporting cast quite good with herbert marshall clifton webb and lucille watson particularly the then famous performance anne baxter however has not worn well one would hope with running time just under two and half hours the film also feels unnecessarily long there seemingly endless cocktail party type banter and indeed the entire india sequence which reads faintly hilarious would have been better cut entirely odd situation for this the very sequence intended the crux the entire film regardless the specific scene all just seems and actual point for the dvd itself the film has not been remastered but the print extremely good and while the bonus package isn particularly memorable neither noticeably poor when all said and done give the razor edge four stars for production values and everyone willingness take the material but frankly this film best left power and tierney fans who will enjoy for the sake the stars and those whose ideas about spiritual enlightenment are vague the film itself gft amazon reviewer
