1 |text richard chamberlain david burton tax lawyer living sydney australia who drawn into murder trial defending five aboriginal men accused murdering fellow native peter weir apocalyptic 1977 thriller the last wave taking where picnic hanging rock left off the film goes deeper into exploring the unknown and the process shows the gulf between two cultures who live side side but lack understanding each others culture and traditions weir shows how white society considers the native beliefs primitive superstitions and believes that since they are living the cities and have been domesticated their tribal laws and culture longer apply from the start burton drawn deeper and deeper into strange web visions and symbols where the line between real time and dream time evaporates water plays important symbolic role the film from the opening sequence which sudden thunder and hailstorm interrupts peaceful school recess burton discovery that his bathtub overflowing and water pouring down his steps violent and unusual weather continue with episodes black rain and mud falling from the sky the contrast between the facile scientific explanations the phenomenon and the intuitive understanding the natives made clear burton and his wife annie olivia hamnet study books about the aborigines and learn about the role dreams the tribal traditions when invites one his clients chris lee david gulpilil his home for dinner disturbed find that the subject inquiry chris and his friend charlie nadjiwarra amagula enigmatic aborigine sorcerer involved with the defendants burton investigation continues his clients make his work difficult refusing disclose the true events surrounding the murder after chris starts appear his dreams burton convinced that the aborigine was killed tribal ritual because saw too much though chris refuses acknowledge this court burton becoming more and more troubled mystery cannot unravel says his stepfather priest why didn you tell there were mysteries this legitimate question but according the reverend the church answers all mysteries burton knows now that must discover the truth for himself and enters the tribal underground caves though not know for certain what real and what dream comes face face with his deepest fears haunting climax that will leave you pondering its meaning into the wee hours the morning this period history which native hopi and mayan prophecies predict the end history and the purification man leading the fifth world the last wave though years old still timely the aborigines are portrayed vibrant culture not one completely subjugated the white man yet troubled the gnawing feeling that are looking but not quite seeing weir has opened our eyes the mystery that lies beyond our consensual view reality but perpetuates the doom orientation that sees possibility only terms fear showing nature dark and uncontrollable power without hint the spiritual beauty that lives both sides time
