-1 |text this film though ostensibly comedy deadly serious its subject imperialism with capital how britain foolishly humiliatingly tries convince itself that still great power after world war home the empire run amiable dolts benevolent tories who are bred that they can distinguish close relatives the offices government consist long forgotten archives dig orwellian paranoia inhabited indolent rats and ante rooms wherein lounge bored synacures reading popular novels abroad britain clings the old pomp but pomp out context looks threadbare and silly especially when its embodied bumbling twits carlton browne unsentimental picture decline with none the lachrymose rot that marred the supposedly anti imperialist jewel the crown the film also about the cold war bravely admitting that dangerous farce whose participants deserve mockery and contempt not fear and respect about how colonialism characterised more neglect than tyranny destroys the colonies deserts robbing them amenities power and most importantly self respect leaving them vulnerable the machinations dangerous cowboys the seriousness course that kills that not say that weighty subjects can treated comedy the miracle morgan creek strangelove and the life brian have all proved that indeed one might suggest that serious themes should only treated comedy allows for clearer eyed view the problem with carlton browne that every situation must have significance beyond the merely comic that becomes weighed down and unfunny the three films mentioned above much the comedy arises from character reaction extreme situation not the extreme situation itself here the script too poor sustain rich comic characterisations and some the greatest comedy talent ever assembled peter sellers terry thomas raymond huntley and john mesurier are criminally wasted terry thomas sublime often shows that couldn handle lead parts and that needed play sneering arrogant bounders not brainless toffs the music made carry much the comedy but its heavy irony only draws attention the lack hilarity screen fair unlike the majority british comedies the period which were stagy and underproduced the boultings often try make their points through film itself montage and composition only huntley manages raise genuine laughs and that essaying character could have played his sleep none the boultings farces have dated well they never thought through enough although carlton browne revels the decline the empire also seems anti democratic and militaristic sure this wasn intended but these blunders are bound happen you allow worthy intentions take precedence over comic intelligence and film form
