1 |text have watched quite few cold case episodes over the years beginning with season episodes back 2003 2004 and while most have been good this particular episode was not only the best the best but has few rivals the emmy categories though some may not agree with the story content the male male romantic relationship doubt that anyone could watch this without being deeply moved within their spirit the story essentially about case that was reopened based the testimony from dying drug dealer the two central actors are two police officers the 1960 named sean coop aka the cold case victim who goes his last name coop and his partner jimmy bruno the story coop single vietnam war vet with deeply troubled past jimmy however married with children less both are partners the police force and form not only friendship but secret romantic relationship that they both must hide from deeply and obviously homophobic culture prevalent that time the flashback scenes their lives are mostly black and white with bits color now and then sprinkled throughout examples include their red squad car the yellow curtains gently blowing the window jimmy bedroom where jimmy wife watched coop and jimmy drink fight and then kiss each other while being alcohol induced state found interesting that only selected items were colored the flashback scenes with everything else black and white still have not figured out the color scheme and rationale the clearly homophobic tension between fellow patrol officers and the two central actors only heightens the intensity the episode one key emotional scene was when coop was confronted his father after the baptism jimmy baby this scene coop father sarge who was respected fellow officer the force confronts coop about the rumors surrounding coop relationship with jimmy one can feel sorry for coop this point the shame and disgrace coop father was heaped upon coop you are not going disgrace our family and you not son either clearly indicative the hostile views same sex relationships that era additional tension can also seen the police locker room where coop and another officer after coop and jimmy are labeled batman and robin homos for the relationship between coop and jimmy obvious that coop wanted more jimmy his life once can see the tension jimmy face must choose between his commitment his wife and kids his church and yet his undying devotion coop the end jimmy walks away from coop realizing that cannot have both coop and his family the same time sadly coop killed perhaps because his relationship with jimmy but coop may also have been killed for his knowledge drug money and police corruption that reached higher the force the most moving scene the whole episode was when coop sat dying from gunshot wounds his squad car quietly spoke his last words over his police radio his partner jimmy were the lucky ones don forget that the soundtrack selection was outstanding throughout the episode enjoyed the final scene with the actor chad everett playing the still grieving jimmy only much older now and clearly still missing his former partner coop highly recommend this episode and consider the best without doubt the most well written well acted and well done all cold case episodes that ever seen
1 |text each the major studios cranked out jazzy one reelers throughout the thirties and forties with universal taking the lead while most looked cheap screen they were make warner bros which abruptly stopped making them 1946 often distinguished theirs with offbeat camera angles mirrors and optical effects thanks some creative directors like jean negulesco fitting that the best this genre should come from this studio what sets jammin the blues apart from the rest the pack that more closely resembles avant garde experiment than hollywood musical filmed july 1944 transforms ordinary jam session into trippy dream escape from war time troubles highlighted the tune the sunny side the street gjon mili and cameraman robert burkes later work with hitchcock were allowed plenty artistic freedom perhaps because lester young was not glenn miller and the studio could care less how and his fellow musicians were presented the optical printer put good use with multiple images the same performer appearing once norman mclaren really milked this process two decades later pas deux while linwood dunn team achieved different effects citizen kane the strong emphasis silhouettes and lit cigarette smoke was also ahead its time some ways this predated the psychedelic sixties but with distinctly forties film noir style
