1 |text this period melodrama one griffith earliest claustrophobic films characters trapped within room are prevalent throughout his work and time went would become increasingly adept portraying their helplessness and involving the audience their terror the bluntly titled sealed room there one major difference the normal plot line that there truly escape griffith achieves the claustrophobic effect here two ways first his use space while the typical biograph short might utilise dozen more sets the sealed room features only two adjoining rooms xc2 x96 the king court and the dove cote that becomes the eponymous tomb the set design these shorts rarely referenced but here crucial the court large interior with backdrop hinting greater depth and showing window and staircase actors enter and leave from various directions suggesting the room not only spacious but also free and open contrast the dove cote back wall very close the camera and the angles suggesting hexagonal octagonal shape make seem even more confined the second technique display here the cross cutting anyone with interest griffith work will probably know about his heavy use and development cross cutting build excitement tension many will also know that strictly speaking wasn his invention however what makes griffith cross cuts effective the way paces the opposing images they complement each other the sealed room contains good example what mean the shots the masons shifting the heavy bricks have slow step step pace them with tension building the wall gets higher this movement matched the shots the blissfully unaware lovers which marion leonard tears off flower petals one one the couple realise their predicament their rising panic complemented the opposing shot the king madly thrashing his sword against the wall this point griffith was yet realise that the action could heightened further introducing third strand the cross cut the dramatic ride the rescue here absent was later become standard climax griffith pictures
