1 |text loosely based the james corbett biography the roar the crowd gentleman jim wonderfully breezy picture that perfectly encapsulates not only the rise the pugilistic prancer that was corbett but also the wind change regards the sport boxing circa the 1890s the story follows corbett perfectly casted errol flynn from his humble beginnings bank teller san fransico thru chance fight with boxing champion that eventually leads him fighting the fearsome heavyweight champion the world john sullivan beefcake personified delightfully ward bond not all the fights are the ring tho and all the spin off vignettes corbett life that makes this grand entertaining picture there are class issues overcome here perfectly played out fellow club members pay have him knocked down peg two and corbett has not only fight get respect from his called peers but must also overcome his ego grows briskly his reputation does along with the quite wonderful corbett family and all their stoic humorous support corbett journey enthralling joyous yet brash and bold very likable character and character that befits the tagged moniker got gentleman jim the film never sags for one moment and testament director raoul walsh that although are eagerly awaiting the final fight the outer ring goings are keeping firmly entertained not even the love interest sub plot hurts this picture thank you alexis smith the fight sequences stand really well and they perfectly show just how corbett became the champ was his brand dancing rings round slugger fighters now firmly placed boxing history the final reel rolls all come down earth after fight meeting between sullivan and corbett puts all the brutality into context and here where humility and humbleness becomes the outright winner and far this viewer goes will for sure sure for truly wonderful picture
