1 |text jean renoir first film whirlpool fate first takes into the world the countryside the rivers the lives the peasantry that will continue explore seems only fitting that his second film deals for the most part with the wealthy and the privileged the upper classes and those who are trying claw their way upwards put the characters from the first two films together and you have the seeds his great grand illusion and rules the game this beautifully filmed with the restless camera making full use the amazingly huge apartments and backstage areas that dominate the film interiors and the acting though frequently overwrought offers some great moments well particularly from werner krauss muffat but the glamorous and sultry hessling who first appears she might give louise brooks run for her money vampishness never goes beyond one note selfish harlot portrayal perhaps this part problem with the script which does seem mostly for high points and outraged emotions not having read the novel not really clear whether the choices were well made not still the differences between nana suitors are well drawn and particularly liked the relationship between muffat and jean angelo vandeuvres the tragic understandings that each seems have his ultimate fate and their sympathy with each other particularly the scene the bottom the enormous staircase where vandeuvres warns muffat and wonder violence will erupt this and other gleanings the ridiculousness the idle rich help give the film the depth has far from his greatest achievement and for probably just shy overall whirlpool fate this still well worth seeing for renoir fans those interested silent cinema generally
