1 |text jeopardy tense satisying thriller cut above but not really major production qualifies almost experimental film the studio that produced metro was desperately looking for new kinds films stars and directors compete with the then new medium television the director john sturges was and comer whose best years lay ahead had just recently begun directing level films and had already proved himself most capable craftsman stars barbara stanwyck barry sullivan and ralph meeker were very different phases their careers stanwyck glory years were behind her and yet she could still carry film she proves here barry sullivan her husband was one dozen leading men who got started films the forties who never quite achieved the success many had hoped for him was fine low key actor poised but upper middle rather than upper class way which made him excellent professional roles the escaped convict who the only person around who can save sullivan life trapped under pier and the tide rising ralph meeker more energetic than usual this excellent actor had the misfortune having come films after brando and clift was his way good actor either them but lacked charisma his bargaining with stanwyck which comes down his demanding sex exchange for saving her husband implication only this 1953 makes for intriguing premise which had this been different kind film could all raised all sorts interesting questions about stanwyck character meeker indeed more exciting character than sullivan and her scenes with him stanwyck livelier than she with her husband and son but this formula picture not strindberg play the possibility that stanwyck might want want have fling leaving aside the question her husband predicament remains unexplored this sense the incoming tide doesn quite have the effect one might have wished though the movie remains tense and highly entertaining thanks excellent acting fine location photography nearly all outdoors and excellent direction the woefully underrated sturges
