1 |text berlin born 1942 margarethe von trotta was actress and now she very important director and writer she has been described perhaps even unfairly caricatured director whose commitment bringing woman sensibility the screen outweighs her artistic strengths rosenstrasse which has garnered mixed and even strange reviews the new york times article was one the most negatively aggressive reviews ever read that paper not perfect film fine movie and testament rare coalescing successful opposition the genocidal nazi regime all peoples generically powerless germans demonstrating berlin street writer von trotta uses the actual rosenstrasse incident the context young woman search for information about her mother never disclosed life child the german capital during world war the husband ruth weinstein jutta lampe has died and surprising reversion orthodox jewish lifestyle apparently hitherto long abeyance ruth not only sits shivah the jews week long mourning ritual but she insists following the strict proscriptions her faith her apartment new york city reflects the affluence secured her deceased spouse labors her american born daughter hannah maria schrader and her brother are bit put off mom assumption restrictive orthodox jewish practices but they pitch the mother coldly rejects the presence hannah fiance non jew named luis fedja van huet domestic crisis might well erupt ruth warns that she disown hannah she doesn give doting handsome luis stay tuned cousin arrives pay her respects and also drops clues interested hannah about wartime mystery about mom childhood berlin hannah intrigued she queries her mom who resolutely refuses discuss that part her life this very very realistic grew with parents who fled nazi germany just time and knew many children whose families whole but usually part escaped the holocaust those days were simply not discussed hannah having learned that german gentile woman saved ruth life traipses off berlin hoping find the savior still breathing were she not this would have been very short film but ruth pretending historian locates year old lena fischer doris schade now widow the happy interviewed but shaken repressed memories lena tells her story the scenes shift fairly seamlessly between present day berlin and the war time capital the young lena 1943 katja riemann was fine pianist married jewish violinist fabian fischer martin feifel with the advent the nazi regime was required use israel middle name just jewish women had add sarah their names incidentally wish imdb had not given fabian name its characters list with the false israel included simply perpetuates name applied nazis mark classification and degradation while germany deported most its jewish population concentration camps those married aryans were exempted for time until 1943 when the regime decided take them too most were men minority were jewish women married non jews the roundup shown here all its frightening intensity the young lena tries locate her husband all she and many other women know that they confined building rosenstrasse the crowd anxious women builds some piteously seeking help from german officers who predictably refuse aid and also verbally abuse them jew loving whore being one appellation subplot lena more less adopts eight year old ruth who hid when her mother was seized remember ruth now sitting shiva manhattan the child ruth fetchingly portrayed svea lohde through increasingly angry protestations the women finally prevail the men and handful women are released the real story the nazis gave one the rare almost unprecedented times when the madmen acknowledged defeat their homicidal agenda another was the termination the euthanasia campaign rid the reich mental defectives and chronic invalids but that another story von trotta builds the tension and each woman story both personal and universal hannah continues prod the aging lena who slowly one gathers begins suspect she not dealing with ordinary historian but rather someone with need learn about the girl she rescued the child whose mother was murdered the contrasts between rosenstrasse 1943 set and the street today bustling rebuilt unified berlin provide recurring thematic element today berlin bears the heritage but not the scars monstrous past von trotta makes that point very well the main actors are uniformly impressive lena husband while strong also shown totally helpless the snare confinement with likely outlook deportation which shown have been clearly understood all characters including the local police and military one way trip oblivion the older ruth catalytically forced confront demons long suppressed her happy new york life hannah very believable young woman whose father death triggers need discover her family past these things happen although the times critic appears not know that von trotta hand sure but not perfect scene with goebbels soiree enjoying lena violin playing unnecessary and distractive the suggestion that she may have gone bed with the propaganda minister the most fanatical top level hitler worshiper save her husband detracts from the wondrous accomplishment the demonstrating spouses and relatives most the german officers come from central casting and are molded the erich von stroheim copy and paste school teutonic nastiness but that understandable the rosenstrasse story has been the subject books and articles and some claim paradigm case for arguing that many more jews could have been saved had more germans protested unfortunately that argument nonsense the german women who occupied rosenstrasse were deeply and understandably self interested most germans were located line somewhere between passive and virulent anti semitism that why the rosenstrasse protest was virtually singular whether one buys rejects the goldenhagen thesis that most germans were willing accomplices the actual murderers just can not denied that pre nazi endemic anti semitism erupted into virulent strain from 1933 the elderly lena remarks that what was accomplished the women was ray light evil time most the men and women sprung from near death trip survived the war ray light was and von trotta movie beacon illumination showing that some were saved the courage largely ordinary women and for every life saved occasion for celebration exists and always will
