1 |text gordon parks the prolific black life magazine photographer made true ticking timebomb movie here one that does not mess around based upon the true story two nyc cops later dubbed batman and robin who singlehandedly employed radical tactics clean their precinct neighborhood drugs this cop buddy movie before that term became repetitive formula lightning paced there not one unimportant throwaway scene here man early 70s nyc must have been terrible place police officer from the looks movies like this and serpico these two cops start out safety division rookies busting dealers plainclothes their spare time but instead receiving applause from the city police department they receive nothing but resistance and antagonism from their peers they have singlehandedly navigate minefield police and legal corruption boneheaded assignments meant keep them from their work the streets ruthless drug kingpins and nasty ghetto neighborhood both david selby and ron leibman are fantastic the leads part the entertainment watching leibman eyes darting around crazily every scene what flawless comic performance and selby acting low key and wry these two make all the comedy aspects the story work displaying palpable frustration mixed with gutsy determination director parks who was already known for his coverage controversial subjects his photography does not shy away from the grittiness the story rather the movie uncompromising portrayal the toughness the world police and streets criminals that these two men inhabit adding this realism the fact that the real hantz and greenberg acted technical advisors for the film and even appear surreal cameo roles two fellow officers who ridicule the protagonists real tribute the effectiveness parks direction that manages perfectly balance this depressing mileu with bright comedy why has mgm let this sit the shelf for years barely giving home video dvd release the minor masterpiece from the 1970s
