1 |text when the british film institute asked martin scorcese create the american part its century the cinema series grabbed the opportunity with both hands personal journey through american movies fascinating wide ranging and the title says highly personal look hollywood cinema scorcese story primarily about hollywood directors xc2 x96 actors producers screenwriters and other collaborators barely get mention states right from the beginning that for him the primary conflict within the film industry that between the director vision and the distributor profit motive between art and commercial viability even opens with clip from vincente minnelli the bad and the beautiful one the earliest films openly explore this contradiction this dictates the structure for the documentary scorcese looks how genres have darkened and clich xc3 xa9s have become challenged how mavericks have challenged the production code and how certain filmmakers fell from grace when they dared different however scorcese never falls into the auteurist trap dismissing directors who consistently pleased the studio bosses lavishes praise cecil mille those who had less recognisable style but were master craftsmen the cinema nonetheless scorcese doesn necessarily focus his absolute favourite directors either orson welles and alfred hitchcock two scorcese biggest influences are only mentioned passing instead looks the individuals and the films that serve tell his story for example shows succession john ford films show how the western evolved looks the work vincente minnelli probably the most often referenced director the documentary show how supposedly wholesome genre like the musical could also have darker undercurrents can imagine that had this assignment not been limited america scorcese would have also loved talk about for example his italian influences his british hero michael powell stretches the definition american movies include both the hollywood films immigrant directors such murnau billy wilder and douglas sirk well the work born filmmakers that was produced elsewhere xc2 x96 such that stanley kubrick rather than simply tell the story hollywood chronologically scorcese compares films from various eras order tackle various subjects his section the language and tools cinema begins with griffith looks the coming sound colour and widescreen and inevitably ends going over computer generated effects which although scorcese not keen them even handed enough include clips george lucas and francis ford coppola defending them however doesn simply finish the chapter here this the end instead then rewinds back the 1940s show how low budget horror like the cat people can achieve effective results from the simplest and cheapest elements personal journey through american movies has one the best film documentaries made there were number outstanding directors and pictures which would never have discovered without and even the most seasoned film buffs would likely find something new its broad scope scorcese has also restored the balance forgotten undervalued pictures was pleased see that when talks about kubrick his iconoclasts chapter looks lolita and barry lindon for his two most underrated films scorcese respect for the medium display the way allows clips play out fully rather than just giving tiny bits and interrupts them with talking heads combination archive and new interviews only when necessary there bit bias towards the 40s and 50s but that hardly surprising since the era which scorcese grew and discovered cinema and after all don think this documentary could have been achieved had not been personal journey one word warning though its depth look certain pictures this documentary does contain fair few spoilers
