1 |text testament gosha incredible film making prowess that was able complete both hitokiri and his stunning masterpiece goyokin the same year 1969 and testament how criminally underrated remains for the general public compared media darlings like the great akira kurosawa that both hitokiri and goyokin have received less than 500 votes between the two them shintaro katsu okada izo mad dog killer loyal the tosha clan and their boss takechi played another genre stalwart tatsuya nakadai the tosha clan was part larger alliance that supported the emperor against the flailing shogunate the historical backdrop fairly accurate with japan increasing political turmoil between imperialists and the tokugawa and the pressure the west end 300 year social and political seclusion helps lot know thing two about japanese history and what eventually led the meiji restoration and the abolition the tokugawa shogunate but not essential any means the movie was made primarily for japanese audience certain things are taken for granted but flows very well for the uninitiated well one would expect from hideo gosha film his golden years the late the visual palette breathtaking the use external and internal symbolism hiding behind pictorial beauty style however never decorative for gosha always employed the service story and speaking story hitokiri dominated both literally and figuratively the tortured main character izo okada most chambara protagonists izo finds himself moral double bind torn between giri obligation and ninjo natural impulse although takes while for him realize what exactly his giri the first half the movie izo trying for social self advancement lofty aspirations social rank and marriage with aristocrat daughter great progression for someone coming from farmer background the rigid social caste system 19th century japan the turning point for izo when realizes what cost self advancement comes the loss identity and consequence the loss self that point that undergoes very symbolic transformation from famous swordsman the tosha clan nameless drifter without past future torazo the vagrant although not technically nameless and not genre drifter yojimbo mold the loss his former self and the cast off ego ambition and self dillusion that allows izo see things they really are and redeem himself hitokiri ends which won reveal here the best way any story can end both positive and negative with deeply ironic twist that gives izo the last laugh last sardonic remark the face death
