-1 |text common practice for film about repression somewhat muted style and tone there difference however between using restraint and encouraging narcolepsy among audience members the secret lives dentists starring campbell scott and hope davis director alan rudolph plays close the vest possible with the result being film that never amounts much beyond rumination how teeth are metaphor for married life scott gives fine performance the role david hurst dentist married another dentist davis rudolph sets the dynamic their relationship quickly completely absorbed the day day duties being the parenthood she quietly disillusioned with their frantic family life and then ratchets the tension when scott may may not witness his wife with another man from this point the film focuses whether not david going confront his wife dana about her possible adultery whether she will beat him the punch and leave for good from time time david treated visits from imaginary friend the form former patient played denis leary borrowing heavily from brad pitt tyler durden fight club while there enough uncertainty about dana infidelity and david instability warrant examination the last two thirds the film are embarrassingly empty theme narrative instead rudolph creates drama out nasty fever that travels slowly through the hurst family culminating pointless hospital visit the film climax the film never picks the hints what david really capable wasn dedicated his family neither does spend much time looking dana precarious balancing act between her family life and her other more fulfilling ambitions choosing spend the majority the film worrying over fever gone awry rudolph kills the momentum his film the time the fifth member the family shows sweating and sickly the film has used all the good graces scott well measured performance david and dana end retracing their steps over and over again until less than cathartic finale with nothing build over the last hour the conclusion seems awkward and patched the secret lives dentists takes common theme and does nothing improve upon altogether disappointing unimaginative film
