1 |text there episode the simpsons which has joke news report referring army training base killbot factory here the comment simply part throwaway joke but what patricia foulkrod documentary does show scarily that not that far from the truth after world war two the army decided tackle problem they faced throughout the war that many soldiers got into battle and found themselves totally unable kill another human being unless was matter them since then the training process the army has been remove all moral scruples and turn recruits into killing machines who don think combatants people develop them most unnatural state the sustainable urge kill first off this isn antiwar movie such whilst certainly paints war very bad light foulkrod focuses rather aspect that doesn get much media attention say the debate over the legality war physical successes failures the affect the process turning man into soldier has that person human being the paradox that train someone soldier defend society makes them totally unsuitable live part that society themselves and whilst most the examples and interviewees are from the current middle east conflict foulkrod makes the links past conflicts especially vietnam painfully clear this isn about any particular war about the problems caused war general structurally the film seems split into three sections how recruits are drawn into the army and the training they receive how they are treated once they are combat and what happens once they leave the army once this point reached you realise that the main target this film actually the policies that are inherent the armed forced policies that are put into place make soldiers into affective combat force but removing all humanity from the individuals those interviewed tell the camera how the recruiting process seems clean and simple how word like democracy and freedom are banded around but once the training begins they become enemy and kill and destroy how once action soldiers don care what they are ordered they are ingrained with the idea that soon they carry out order whatever may they are one step closer going home they have political social ideals fight for but fight and kill that what they been trained but the ground truth main goal highlight the way the army discards those who have fought for their country once they return home there real rehabilitation given soldiers returning and many are forced home unable cope with what they have seen and done and most policies place seem make sure the army has legal responsibility whatsoever for psychological affects their soldiers pick this the final indignity that once they are used they are cast away there flaw the film that foulkrod doesn attempt show another side the argument you would get the impression that every single soldier who ever went war would come back with post traumatic stress syndrome would have been interesting see those xc2 x85 less liberal upbringing give their opinions how the army handles training and policies there never chance for the other side the argument make itself known but other than that this expertly crafted documentary and foulkrod use stock footage and music perfectly utilised get across side war that too often get passed when discussing the fallout war
