1 |text child always hated being forced sit through musicals never understood why people would break out into song like that and was far too young appreciate the artistry choreography set design costumes pacing behind all carol reed oliver was the one musical remember oddly enjoying child probably because one the darker ones and appropriately drenched the spirit dickensian squalor this musical about ghetto life victorian london and while the scenery and set designs are stark dark and true that way life flat out bizarre for people breaking out into such ridiculous songs amidst their misery upon recent viewing first since childhood have some new thoughts and insights into why this musical works that bizarre breaking out into song kind way and why most just don for when musicals work really say something because they realize their own inherent strangeness lars von trier dancer the dark tragic and operatic and over reaching was worked musical because the musical numbers were the products the imagination the protagonist immigrant obsessed with hollywood musicals likewise the very cynical and enjoyable chicago worked similar level because the musical numbers were the products homicidal ingenue singer dancer musicals don work when they take their own musical nature too seriously like moulin rouge are simply too much fluff about nothing something pointless like mary poppins upon viewing oliver for the first time adult saw new light told mostly from the point view young oliver saw the musical numbers the products his childhood imagination and his way coping with the horrors ghetto life around him the best musical number was probably when nancy got everyone the tavern signing and dancing about the joys getting drunk cover help poor oliver escape the clutches the evil bill sykes was undeniably catchy and sounded like real pub tune that drunks might start singing around piano there are other great and classic tunes heard here and the direction and acting from the leads the dancing extras are all top notch still for all its bleakness although does have happy ending for oliver least though certainly things didn end happily for nancy and unless you think life the streets being pick pocket fun wasn necessarily good ending for fagin the dodger despite their peppy closing tune wouldn really classify this family film though don think showing kids over the age seven eight will any harm this harsh tale about unfortunate orphan trying survive the streets and find some happiness think would very interesting see modern update this some how perhaps revisionist take where people the streets compton break into happy songs about their horrible lives like see hard edged hip hop version oliver always thought dickens would translate well those regards stands oliver was probably the last the great film musicals and maybe the strangest rated film ever seen
