1 |text still the definitive program about the second world war the world war isn just long but also very informative the series contains episodes each episode lasts for about min and includes the events leading and following the wake the war most episodes are about the war europe and there are several episodes about the war the pacific other episodes include information about the wars africa burma the atlantic and the home fronts germany great britain united states and soviet union there one episode that dedicated the holocaust the series starts off with the episode new germany 1933 1939 and tells about the rise the nazis germany and german territorial gains prior the outbreak war the series ends with the episode remember the war influence post war world remember fitting episode end this great program every episode begins with short introduction and then with opening credits the credits are accompanied powerful music theme there are many fitting music pieces throughout the series each episode like mini film the footage fantastic and the way was put together addition some the footage color the information included also makes the episodes memorable and entertaining the series was produced jeremy isaacs for thames television commissioned 1969 took four years produce such was the depth its research the series was narrated laurence olivier one the most famous and revered actors the 20th century the series interviewed leading members the allied and axis campaigns including eyewitness accounts civilians enlisted men officers and politicians amongst them albert speer karl donitz jimmy stewart bill mauldin curtis lemay lord mountbatten alger hiss toshikazu kase arthur harris charles sweeney paul tibbets traudl junge and historian stephen ambrose jeremy isaacs says the making the world war that sought interview not necessarily the surviving big names but their aides and assistants the most difficult subject locate and persuade interviewed according isaacs was heinrich himmler adjutant karl wolff the latter admitted witnessing large scale execution himmler presence the world war often considered the definitive television history the second world war some consider the finest example the documentary form list the 100 greatest british television programmes drawn the british film institute 2000 voted for industry professionals the world war ranked 19th the program has everything that the viewer needs know about the war after watching few episodes liked the series much that tried watch the remaining episodes one after the other seen some them several times there are two other great documentary series that know that may interest the viewer one called the great war 1964 that about world war the other called cold war 1998 that about the cold war obviously
