1 |text the late 30s and early 40s were golden age for adventure movies what with the rise budgets during the economic recovery the changes screen entertainment since the production code became enforced and the general carefree optimism the times while most these were rip roaring swashbucklers about the wild superhuman and often frankly misogynistic exploits heartthrobs like errol flynn and tyrone power gunga din very different its focus scope and tone part gunga din secret the division labour its writing team the original story ben hecht and charles macarthur two the most skilled and celebrated writers hollywood golden age however the actual screenplay was the work joel sayre and fred guiol both whom guiol especially had background comedy what get from these four plot that well balanced and engaging yet also cleverly spiced with comical touches most the adventure flicks this time were least partly comedies usually featuring one two comic relief supporting players but they didn use laughs the way gunga din does here all the main characters are capable being objects originators jokes see the sinister menace the bad guys suddenly diffused the scene dissolves into light hearted brawl the first main battle scene even handed blend action and gags the style the silent swashbucklers douglas fairbanks something which the flynn and power vehicles largely failed replicate towards the middle gunga din the action must necessarily take break and there are lots talky scenes for the sake the plot however the continual forays into comedy xc2 x96 such the spiked punch routine xc2 x96 make this slow portion bearable producer director george stevens was natural when came this sort thing himself having cut his teeth the hal roach studios and almost exclusively having directed comedy this point this was his first full action feature and does startlingly good job particular his use moving point view shots make the battle scenes extra exhilarating also brings something you seldom see action pictures this era xc2 x96 sense real dread and fear sets this with those stark and foreboding mountains dominating many the shots and dwarfing the characters the portrayal the abandoned village and the thuggee cultists cry kali genuinely haunting this dimension fear plays into all the other emotions that are work here causing worry for these likable characters and making the comedy greater relief tension real touch genius the way the eponymous hero introduced the audience are made aware din visually prominent number scenes before any the characters actually address him verbally refer him because this are given the impression that din not important figure within the regiment but quickly becomes notable character and crucially sympathetic one see him risking his life and giving water dying men but the best efforts writers and directors are all for nought without capable cast fear not for gunga din has top notch one victor mclaglan and cary grant were ideally suited the material since their best roles were generally found somewhere the spectrum between drama and comedy grant particular his best largely believable but just occasionally breaking into that over the top whooping and capering that was his trademark douglas fairbanks not quite the standard his heavyweight companions but means bad and course there sam jaffe cursed his looks forever play these wizened little oddballs but who else could play them with such dignity and humanity have not set out bash the swashbuckling adventures errol flynn and tyrone power and indeed many their pictures are absolute classics that love absolutely but gunga din does things that even the best those swashbucklers could never achieve not only does dispense with the dashing male lead the clich xc3 xa9d defiant damsel successfully merges the action genre with comedy and poignancy way that few pictures have done before since and that fabulous
