1 |text written the wind directed douglas sirk and released 1956 like all sirk mid films pure melodrama yet engrossing richly developed melodrama and sirk trademark lurid colour expressionism throbbing barely repressed emotions symbolism and juxtaposition the classes make this film crave the film opens brilliantly with the four central characters and the plot being introduced the credits are still rolling sirk uses clever flashback structure take into his world robert stack and dorothy malone are magnificent the two hadley kids kyle and marylee drinks and sleeps around with women she drinks and sleeps around with men they both are worth millions thanks the hadley oil business hunky yet poor mitch wayne rock hudson kyle lifelong friend and marylee dream lover enter into this sordid mess lucy moore lauren bacall slim attractive young woman who falls under kyle charms after picks phone and flies her across the countryside one evening mitch loves her too but kyle wins her they quickly marry and kyle stops drinking but fate seems written the wind and not long before conniving marylee who will have mitch marriage marriage secretly smitten mitch the confused lucy and the sad drunk kyle come blows malone just wonderful marylee hadley thoroughly deserving her best supporting actress oscar she steals every scene she stack almost just good amping the melodrama while still maintaining subtly and quiet desperation hudson and bacall are lot more restrained than those two yet keeping with the characters they play what all this melodrama really about well lot things stack powerful portrait male inadequacy and fear for one thing sirk surrounds stack with phallic symbols throughout the film note his tiny little gun the oil derricks and the ultimate phallic symbol kyle seeming inability conceive children stack seems suffering from massive male superiority complex made worse his father preference for hudson his sister desire for hudson and his suspicion that his wife carrying with hudson with all this wealth kyle hadley still ends the wrong end town buying cheap corn liquor like bum about impossible dreams and having let them the river where kyle marylee and mitch used play when they were kids constantly referenced throughout the film symbolising kyle and especially marylee wish for the innocence and simplicity youth excellent melodramatic scene perfectly pulled off malone marylee stands the river and imagines herself again child with voice over mitch telling marylee that she will always his girl this where sirk strikes huge emotional chord with the viewer who hasn dreamed about going back that special place childhood who hasn some point lived treasured memory who hasn wanted something they couldn have and hudson last line the film yes gets dialogue the last minutes only close ups recollects how far come from the river marylee amazing
