1 |text apart from having the longest reign british history years queen victoria also holds two other distinctions she was apart from our current queen the oldest ever british monarch living the age and she was also the youngest ever british opposed english scottish monarch coming the throne girl eighteen and yet whenever television the cinema make programme film about her they seem far more interested the older victoria than they the young girl the version victoria with which modern audiences will probably most familiar judi dench mrs brown the young victoria tries redress the balance showing the events surrounding her accession and the early years her reign has the rare distinction being produced former royal sarah duchess york whose daughter princess beatrice makes brief appearance extra there are three main strands the plot the first concerns the intrigues victoria mother the duchess kent highly unpopular figure even with her own daughter largely because the influence her adviser sir john conroy who was widely rumoured her lover according one unfounded rumour and not the late duke kent was victoria natural father the second strand concerns the growing romance between victoria and her german cousin prince albert and the attempts king leopold belgium who was uncle both them influence this romance leopold hope was increase the prestige the house saxe coburg which both and albert belonged the third concerns one the strangest episodes british political history the bedchamber crisis 1839 when supporters the tory party which had traditionally supported strong monarchy rioted because the young queen was perceived favour the whig party and their leader lord melbourne even though the whigs had historically supported quasi republican system government with the monarch reduced figurehead scriptwriter julian fellowes known for his conservative views and times wondered this may have coloured his treatment political themes seems lean the side the tories the predecessors the modern conservative party their leader robert peel shown statesmanlike and dignified whereas melbourne for all his dash and charm shown devious and uninterested social reform there may some truth these characterisations but fellowes glosses over the fact that only few years earlier the tories had opposed the reform act which ended the corrupt electoral system rotten boroughs and that they had benefited from william unconstitutional dismissal whig administration lessons dynastic and constitutional history not always transfer well the cinema screen and this one contains its share inaccuracies prince albert for example was not injured edward oxford attempt victoria life and melbourne his late fifties the time victoria accession was not youthful portrayed here paul bettany king william certainly disliked the duchess kent who was his sister law but doubt would have gone far bawl abuse her during state banquet shown doing here also failed understand the significance the scene which the duchess and conroy try force victoria sign regency order the duchess constitutional position was made clear the regency act 1830 which provided that she would become regent her daughter was still under eighteen the time her accession piece paper signed victoria could have altered the provisions the act there are also occasional infelicities one early scene see victoria and albert playing chess while comparing themselves pawns being moved around chessboard metaphor hackneyed that the whole scene should have come complete with danger major clich xc3 xa9 ahead warning yet spite scenes like this came enjoy the film there were some good performances especially from miranda richardson the scheming duchess and mark strong the obnoxious conroy visually very attractive being shot sumptuous style have come associate with british historical drama jim broadbent gives amusing turn king william although does occasionally succumb the temptation going over the top although not disastrously over the top was moulin rouge the main reason for the film success however the performances emily blunt and rupert friend the two young lovers victoria and albert blunt probably more attractive than victoria was real life but her delightful portrayal the queen longer the old lady the popular imagination the black clad widow windsor who was perpetually not amused but determined strong minded and loving young woman her love for albert and their happy family life together was one the main reasons why the monarchy succeeded reestablishing itself the affections the british people with the exception george iii victoria hanoverian ancestors had been notoriously lacking the matrimonial virtues blunt and friend make the young victoria touching romance and gripping human drama well exploration key period british history
