1 |text sucker for films like this films that take you back and let you relive your childhood grown now and have many grown responsibilities like mortgage kids dogs wife and slew others enjoy life but not innocent and carefree like was when was twelve mike binder indian summer knows this and explores this like was twelve years old brings you back time when life was simpler and much more fun brings you back time when worrying about your first kiss and wondering you could finish the camp marathon were important issues indian summer fantastic film and one that should watched least once year just you can sit back and laugh and reminisce the film stars kevin pollak bill paxton diane lane and matt craven name few childhood friends that are being summoned back camp tamakwa their former head camp counsellor uncle lou uncle lou played perfectly alan arkin kind guy who the patriarch the group also all knowing and encompasses the true spirit father figure and someone who understands the simple things life has hard time relating today kids that need walkman blaring their ears when they are place immense beauty like tamakwa this camp that has moose wandering through the camp leaves turning colours that god gave them and water for far the eye can see uncle lou yearns for the days old and asks his former campers back the camp see one them will take over the camp while they are all together again get see their trials and tribulations and perhaps new love could spring between them the adults return the camp isn long before they act like kids again the typical camp pranks get played all over again they take toilet paper out the stalls the put toothpaste sleeping bags and all this done hilariously and with actors like pollak and paxton all very funny stuff but beyond the hilarity get explore some very real adult emotion that anyone can relate one favourite scenes kevin pollak and elizabeth perkins are overlooking bay where they used canoing kids pollak can get over how small all looks and perkins finally tells him that the bay didn get smaller they just got bigger doesn hammer the point home but does subtly all grow all move and all unfortunately can live like did years ago the more things change the more they stay the same indian summer character driven film and written beautifully mike binder who actually did attend camp camp tamakwa did sam raimi who played stick the film and his fond and vivid memories his experiences that fuel the film there are many touching scenes and there are many hilarious ones also both are perfect love this film love everything about and true hidden gem
