1 |text with iphigenia mikhali cacoyannis perhaps the first film director have successfully brought the feel ancient greek theatre the screen his own screenplay adaptation euripides tragedy was far from easy compared that the other two films the trilogy directed the story has been very carefully deconstructed from euripides version and placed logical strictly chronological framework better conforming the modern methods cinematic story telling cacoyannis also added some characters his film that not appear euripides tragedy odysseus calchas and the army this was done order make some euripides points regarding war the church and government clearer finally cacoyannis iphigenia ending somewhat ambiguous when compared euripides the film was shot location aulis the director photography giorgos arvanitis shows rugged but beautiful greece where since the homeric days time seems have stood still takes advantage the bodies the arid land the ruins the intense light and the darkness the harshness the landscape particularly fitting the souls the characters the camera uses the whole gamut available shots from the very long reinforcing the vastness and desolation the landscape well the human scale involved the extreme close ups dissecting and probing deep into the soul the tormented characters particular the film opening with bold accelerating tracking shot along line beached boats followed aerial view the many thousands soldiers lying listlessly the beach very effective means communicating agamemnon awesome political and military responsibility word but sublime can describe the stunning performances costa kazakos agamemnon irene papas clytemnestra and tatiana papamoschou iphigenia kazakos and papas embody the sublimity the classical greece tragedy kazakos character extremely down earth and his powerful look into the camera more than his words reveals the unbelievable torment tearing his soul irene papas the modern quintessence classic greek plays iphigenia she terrible her anguish and even more for what know will her vengeance tatiana papamoskou her first role the screen outstanding her portray the innocent iphigenia which contrasts with kazakos austere depiction her father agamemnon cacoyannis faithful euripides his representation the other characters odysseus sly scheming politician achilles vain narcissistic warrior menalaus self centered obsessed with his honor eager avenged and have his wife and property restored the costumes and sets are realistic hollywood there agamemnon quarters resembles barn dresses the others utilitarian hand woven simple garb clytemnestra royal caravan made rough hewn wooden carts the music the prolific contemporary music composer mikis theodorakis theodorakis score intensifies the dramatic and cinematographic unfolding reflects the psychological aspect the tragedy and accentuates its dimensions and actuality this film and the story narrates offer considerable insight into the lost world ancient greek thought that was the crucible for much our modern civilization teaches much about ourselves individuals and social and political creatures euripides questions the value war and patriotism when measured against the simple virtues family and love and reflects woman vulnerable position world manly violence his adaptation euripides tragedy cacoyannis revisits all these themes modern clear and dramatic fashion the relationships governing the political machinations are clearly demonstrated war corrupts and destroys the human soul such extent that neither the individual nor the group can function normally any longer with the possible exception menelaus whose honor has been tarnished his own wife elopement with her lover everyone else has his own private motivation for going war with troy which has nothing with helen the thirst for power agamemnon greed the army odysseus glory achilles and real sense helen became the wmd the trojan war the war stripped all homeric glamor and religious sanctioning was just imperialist venture spurred primarily the desire for material gain all else being convenient pretext another conflict raised the film that between the church and the state calchas who represents the church feeling the challenge his priestly authority and wishing destroy agamemnon for the insult the goddess serves tells him sacrifice his daughter consenting the sacrifice the king comes closer his moral undoing but refusing loses his power over the masses his army who are brainwashed religion course for agamemnon game the king must along with the charade whether honestly believes the gods not until realizes too late that has ensnared himself into committing despicable filicide sacrifice murder and how can tell the difference between the two focusing the violent and primitive horror human sacrifice and worst all the sacrifice one own child euripides cacoyannis creates drama that once deeply political and agonizingly personal touches most complex and delicate ethical problem facing any society the dire conflict between the needs the individual versus those the society the case iphigenia however the biblical tale abraham and isaac the father asked kill his own child his own hand what sort god would insist such payment can just moral even divinely inspired finally does the daughter sacrificial death differ from the deaths all the sons and daughters who are being sent war these are many deep questions raised two hour film
