1 |text the matador dark comedy starring pierce brosnan aging hit man who befriends straight arrow happily married american businessman greg kinnear mexico although the sardonic julian ironically surnamed noble appears tough guy the surface underneath really just mass neuroses and insecurities man who realizes that his chosen profession has left him virtually alone the world and friendless thus his birthday reaches out danny man who has lived his life playing the rules and who becomes strangely intrigued julian unconventional lifestyle fact both men find each other the person they could never but wish they could become their quieter franker moments self evaluation although the film bit too reminiscent analyze this and midnight run feel entirely fresh and original the matador nevertheless earns points for the complexity its characters and the quality its acting brosnan looking aged and almost used sinks his teeth into the role julian way never has before playing man who seems constitutionally unable make serious connection with another human being brosnan flip and cavalier one moment then genuine and sympathetic the next always keeps off balance that the humor never becomes crass stale kinnear also excellent the regular joe businessman who finds himself pulled ever more the dark side his relationship with julian deepens hope davis does fine job bean danny loyal and loving wife who like her husband finds herself intrigued this mysterious and dangerous figure from world far different from the one she knows times find ourselves feeling that the matador holding something back from not quite plumbing the depths its situation and premise the end feels little too light little too insubstantial register the impact should this could because this the feature film debut for writer director richard shephard and hasn quite gotten his movie pacing down yet still one appreciates the fact that doesn always for the obvious and that keeps tightly focused the two main figures the piece rarely settling for the trite setup the easy laugh the matador flawed but generally entertaining little comedy that will hopefully signal new phase brosnan acting career
